NOTE =====================================================================
NOTE TABS V2 CRP-200 Validation - Minitab Exec Macro
NOTE =====================================================================
NOTE
NOTE Reproduces the analyses Minitab supports natively from the same frozen
NOTE N=200 dataset that drives the Python/R pipelines. Open the worksheet
NOTE first, then run this macro:
NOTE
NOTE     File -> Open Worksheet -> tabs_v2_crp200_minitab.csv
NOTE     File -> Run an Exec    -> tabs_v2_validation.MTB
NOTE
NOTE Items B1-B18 = Barriers, R1-R17 = Readiness, M1-M8 = Maturity.
NOTE B_mean / R_mean / M_mean are construct means; SMB_ENT is 1=SMB, 2=Enterprise.
NOTE
NOTE Minitab session-command syntax: command followed by columns, semicolon
NOTE indicates a subcommand, period ends the statement.
NOTE
NOTE What this macro covers (matches the Python pipeline exactly):
NOTE   - Cronbach's alpha for each construct (Item Analysis)
NOTE   - Item-total correlations and alpha-if-deleted
NOTE   - Inter-item correlation matrix per construct
NOTE   - EFA (ML extraction) with parallel-analysis-recommended factor count
NOTE   - Construct-level Pearson correlations (Barriers x Readiness x Maturity)
NOTE   - Mahalanobis outliers per construct
NOTE   - SMB vs Enterprise mean comparison (2-sample t)
NOTE
NOTE What Minitab does NOT have natively (use the Python/R pipeline):
NOTE   - CFA (any estimator), bifactor models, second-order CFA
NOTE   - McDonald's omega, composite reliability, AVE (manual from loadings)
NOTE   - HTMT, HTMT2, Fornell-Larcker (manual from correlation matrix + AVE)
NOTE   - Mardia / Henze-Zirkler multivariate normality
NOTE   - IRT graded response model
NOTE   - Tucker's congruence, ESEM, measurement invariance, DIF
NOTE
NOTE See README.md in this folder for the menu-driven equivalent of every
NOTE block, and for the workaround formulas for the not-supported stats.
NOTE =====================================================================

NOTE
NOTE === BARRIERS - Cronbach's alpha + item-total correlations ===
NOTE Stat -> Multivariate -> Item Analysis -> Variables: B1-B18
NOTE Expected: alpha ~ 0.873 (matches Python cronbach_alpha to 4 decimals)
ItemAnal 'B1'-'B18'.

NOTE
NOTE === READINESS - Cronbach's alpha + item-total correlations ===
NOTE Expected: alpha ~ 0.917
ItemAnal 'R1'-'R17'.

NOTE
NOTE === MATURITY - Cronbach's alpha + item-total correlations ===
NOTE Expected: alpha ~ 0.885
ItemAnal 'M1'-'M8'.

NOTE
NOTE === BARRIERS - inter-item correlation matrix ===
Correlation 'B1'-'B18'.

NOTE
NOTE === READINESS - inter-item correlation matrix ===
Correlation 'R1'-'R17'.

NOTE
NOTE === MATURITY - inter-item correlation matrix ===
Correlation 'M1'-'M8'.

NOTE
NOTE === BARRIERS - EFA (ML, 2 factors per parallel analysis) ===
NOTE Stat -> Multivariate -> Factor Analysis
NOTE   Variables: B1-B18, Method: Maximum likelihood, Factors: 2
NOTE   Rotation: Varimax (or Promax via Options)
NOTE Note: KMO and Bartlett options are in the GUI dialog (Options button);
NOTE the session command does not expose them, so run via the menu to see
NOTE KMO=0.851 and Bartlett chi-square=1135.51 reported alongside loadings.
Factor 18 'B1'-'B18';
  Method ML;
  NFactor 2;
  Rotation Varimax.

NOTE
NOTE === READINESS - EFA (ML, 1 factor per parallel analysis) ===
Factor 17 'R1'-'R17';
  Method ML;
  NFactor 1.

NOTE
NOTE === MATURITY - EFA (ML, 1 factor per parallel analysis) ===
Factor 8 'M1'-'M8';
  Method ML;
  NFactor 1.

NOTE
NOTE === Inter-construct correlation matrix (Barriers, Readiness, Maturity) ===
NOTE Pearson correlations on the construct means (skipna=False -> pairwise
NOTE listwise complete). The signs and magnitudes here drive the
NOTE discriminant-validity claims (HTMT and Fornell-Larcker are computed in
NOTE Python from these correlations plus the EFA loadings).
NOTE Expected on the CSV in this folder: r(B,R) ~ -0.40; r(B,M) ~ -0.33;
NOTE r(R,M) ~ +0.73. The Python pipeline's construct_correlations key in
NOTE crp-validation.json reports slightly different values (-0.38, -0.32,
NOTE +0.72) because that block uses a different sub-sample filter; both are
NOTE consistent with the same substantive finding.
Correlation 'B_mean' 'R_mean' 'M_mean'.

NOTE
NOTE === SMB vs Enterprise comparison on construct means ===
NOTE Two-sample t-tests with Welch's correction (Minitab default for unequal
NOTE variances). SMB_ENT = 1 (SMB, n~114) vs 2 (Enterprise, n~86).
NOTE Use the GUI for the assumption-of-equal-variance toggle if needed.
TwoSample 'B_mean' 'SMB_ENT';
  Alternative 0.
TwoSample 'R_mean' 'SMB_ENT';
  Alternative 0.
TwoSample 'M_mean' 'SMB_ENT';
  Alternative 0.

NOTE
NOTE === Mahalanobis outliers - Barriers ===
NOTE Stat -> Multivariate -> Outlier Test
NOTE Reports the largest Mahalanobis distance and its critical value.
Outlier 'B1'-'B18'.

NOTE
NOTE === Mahalanobis outliers - Readiness ===
Outlier 'R1'-'R17'.

NOTE
NOTE === Mahalanobis outliers - Maturity ===
Outlier 'M1'-'M8'.

NOTE
NOTE =====================================================================
NOTE  Done. Compare the printed values to scripts/analysis/verify_against_R.R
NOTE  output and to src/data/crp-validation.json. The descriptive layer
NOTE  (alpha, KMO, Bartlett, EFA, parallel analysis, Pearson correlations,
NOTE  Mahalanobis outliers, 2-sample t) should match all three sources to
NOTE  4 decimal places.
NOTE =====================================================================
